<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#perspectivallyContradicting> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates two dimensions offering different viewpoints on another dimension in memes, e.g. same visual content from different perspectives"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dramaticallyIronicalTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates one dimension revealing information another dimension does not provide in memes, e.g. text revealing information visuals don't show"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01264#audience_id> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme is designed for a broad, general internet audience familiar with animal puns and self-deprecating humor. It relies on common cultural knowledge of the word 'nuts' as a colloquial term for crazy or eccentric, and the visual association of squirrels with that term. The humor is lighthearted and non-harmful, making it accessible to most internet users without requiring specific demographic, cultural, or community-specific knowledge beyond basic English language comprehension and familiarity with internet meme formats."@en .
