<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01264#overallintent_instance> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "meme-id: youre_a_special_kinda_nuts; hasOverallIntent: overall-intent_id; overall-intent-description: The image features a squirrel sitting on a tree branch with the text overlay 'you're a special kinda nuts aren't you?'. The scene is a harmless, natural setting with no contextual markers of toxicity. The meme uses a pun on 'nuts' (meaning nutty or crazy) and the animal's natural behavior to create a lighthearted, self-deprecating joke. There is no abusive, cyberbullying, harmful, hateful, misogynous, or propaganda content. The intent is purely humorous and relies on wordplay and visual juxtaposition (the squirrel's appearance mapped onto the pun about 'nuts'). The meme is intended to elicit laughter through a harmless pun, not to target or harm any group or individual."@en .
_:n4c986ac4ba6c41688210d6715d29faa3b41 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An ontology for analyzing memes including textual and visual materials with their interrelations"@en .
_:n4c986ac4ba6c41688210d6715d29faa3b39 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#VisualMaterial> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#prototypicalQuestions> "1. What visual elements are present in this meme? (Identify all images, graphics, illustrations, photographs, symbols, or visual components)\n\n2. What is the source or origin of the visual content? (Consider whether images are from movies, TV shows, stock photos, original artwork, screenshots, etc.)\n\n3. How are the visual elements composed and arranged within the frame? (Examine layout, positioning, size relationships, and visual balance)\n\n4. What colors, lighting, and visual style characterize the meme? (Analyze color schemes, contrast, brightness, saturation, and overall aesthetic)\n\n5. What emotions or reactions do the visual elements convey? (Consider facial expressions, body language, visual mood, and atmospheric qualities)\n\n6. Are there recognizable people, characters, objects, or settings in the visuals? (Identify any familiar faces, fictional characters, locations, or iconic imagery)\n\n7. What visual techniques or effects have been applied? (Look for editing, filters, distortion, overlays, scaling, or other modifications)\n\n8. How do the visual elements relate to each other within the meme? (Examine visual connections, contrasts, parallels, or interactions between different visual components)\n\n9. What cultural or symbolic meanings are embedded in the visual content? (Consider visual metaphors, symbolic representations, or culturally significant imagery)\n\n10. How do the visual elements support or create the meme's intended message? (Analyze how the imagery contributes to humor, commentary, or communicative purpose)" .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01264#animal_pun_meme_format> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#BackgroundKnowledge> .
